oled over the long run.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 57\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>57\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 65\u00b0F, and the record is 84\u00b0F (1931). The low of <strong>35\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Record highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Greenville's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Greenville has set <strong>51 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 80 that chance alone would give, and <strong>61 record highs</strong>, close to the 80 that chance alone would give. The 1910s hold the most record highs of any decade (53). Greenville's all-time high is 110\u00b0F, on July 29, 1930.","t_chg":"Greenville's nights have warmed since 1977. Its days cooled over the long run.","p_chg":"Across 110 complete years: Greenville's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.14) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Greenville's average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.12\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.20 to \u22120.03), about \u22121.4\u00b0F since 1903. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1977, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.46\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.22 to +0.70) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 3 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; January fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Greenville's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.32 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 49.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 2019 (78.17 in); the driest, 2010 (28.90 in). Greenville almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.2 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}